## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

### 1. Reference Equality

React re-renders a component when its props or state change. The comparison is **shallow** (`===`), so primitives compare by value, but objects/arrays/functions compare by **reference**.

Every render creates new references for inline definitions:

```jsx
// New object reference on every render → child always re-renders
<Child config={{ theme: 'dark' }} />

// New function reference on every render → child always re-renders
<Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />
```

Even if the *contents* are identical, `{} !== {}` and `() => {} !== () => {}`.

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

| Tool | What it memoizes | Use when |
|---|---|---|
| `React.memo` | A component's render output | Child receives stable props but re-renders due to parent |
| `useMemo` | A computed value | Expensive calculation; or object/array passed as prop |
| `useCallback` | A function reference | Function passed as prop to a memoized child |

**`React.memo`** — wraps a component; skips re-render if props are shallowly equal:
```jsx
const Child = React.memo(({ onClick, label }) => <button onClick={onClick}>{label}</button>);
```

**`useMemo`** — stable object/array reference across renders:
```jsx
const config = useMemo(() => ({ theme: 'dark', size: 'lg' }), []);
```

**`useCallback`** — stable function reference across renders:
```jsx
const handleClick = useCallback(() => doSomething(id), [id]);
```

> **Don't over-memoize.** These tools have overhead. Profile first; memoize only proven hot paths or props fed into `React.memo` children.

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

Context re-renders **every consumer** when the provider's `value` changes reference:

```jsx
// Bad: new object on every parent render
<UserContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>
```

```jsx
// Good: memoize the value
const contextValue = useMemo(() => ({ user, setUser }), [user, setUser]);
<UserContext.Provider value={contextValue}>
```

Also, split frequently-changing context from rarely-changing context so consumers only re-render when relevant data changes.

---

### 4. Before / After Example

**Before — unnecessary re-renders:**
```jsx
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Increment</button>
      {/* New function + new object on every render → ExpensiveChild always re-renders */}
      <ExpensiveChild
        onAction={() => console.log('action')}
        style={{ color: 'red' }}
      />
    </div>
  );
}

function ExpensiveChild({ onAction, style }) {
  console.log('ExpensiveChild rendered'); // fires on every Parent render
  return <div style={style}><button onClick={onAction}>Go</button></div>;
}
```

**After — re-renders suppressed:**
```jsx
// Wrap with memo so React skips render when props are shallowly equal
const ExpensiveChild = React.memo(function ExpensiveChild({ onAction, style }) {
  console.log('ExpensiveChild rendered'); // only fires when onAction or style actually change
  return <div style={style}><button onClick={onAction}>Go</button></div>;
});

function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  // Stable function reference; only recreated if deps change
  const handleAction = useCallback(() => console.log('action'), []);

  // Stable object reference; only recreated if deps change
  const style = useMemo(() => ({ color: 'red' }), []);

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Increment</button>
      <ExpensiveChild onAction={handleAction} style={style} />
    </div>
  );
}
```

Incrementing `count` no longer re-renders `ExpensiveChild` because `handleAction` and `style` keep their references stable across renders.
